<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to main content</a>

  <header>
    <div class="inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">constable</p>
      <h1>Community-led supports for a better everyday life</h1>
      <p class="deck">
        constable is a new disability-led research project examining how peer support,
        practical access improvements, and participant-controlled services can improve
        autonomy, participation, belonging, and quality of life. The project will use
        the atlantis research commons to support inclusive engagement and responsible data sharing.
      </p>
      <div class="project-meta" aria-label="Project summary">
        <span>30 months</span>
        <span>720 participants</span>
        <span>12 communities</span>
        <span>Disability-led governance</span>
        <span>Mixed-methods evaluation</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="callout" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
      <h2 id="summary-heading">Executive summary</h2>
      <p>
        Disabled people are frequently asked to adapt to services designed without them.
        This can create unnecessary barriers to employment, education, transportation,
        healthcare, relationships, leisure, and community participation. Evidence about
        what improves quality of life is strongest when outcomes are selected by disabled
        people themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        The proposed constable project will test a community-based support package
        across 12 communities in six diverse regions. The package will combine paid peer
        navigation, small grants for removing practical barriers, accessibility improvements,
        and participant-designed quality-of-life action plans. Six communities will receive
        the package early and six will receive it after a nine-month delay, allowing the
        research team to compare outcomes while ensuring that every community eventually
        receives the intervention.
      </p>
      <p>
        The atlantis research commons will provide accessible study information, consent
        tools, data collection, participant feedback, and a community knowledge hub.
        Findings will be reported in plain language, Easy Read, audio, visual, and accessible
        digital formats.
      </p>
      <div class="principle">
        Central principle: nothing about disabled people without disabled people—including
        how research questions, measures, success, and findings are defined.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="need-heading">
      <h2 id="need-heading">1. Rationale and research gap</h2>
      <p>
        Public programs often measure success through referral completion, program
        attendance, or administrative targets. These indicators do not necessarily show
        whether disabled people have greater choice, control, safety, confidence, or
        access to ordinary community life. They may also overlook barriers that are not
        resolved by information and referral alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        constable addresses three gaps:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Participation gap:</strong> Disabled people, particularly people with
          intellectual disabilities, people who are non-verbal, and people from
          under-served communities, are often excluded from research design and data collection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Implementation gap:</strong> Programs frequently offer information
          without addressing transport, inaccessible venues, communication barriers,
          discrimination, or the administrative burden of obtaining support.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measurement gap:</strong> Standard surveys may be inaccessible,
          burdensome, or insufficiently connected to what participants consider a good life.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The project is grounded in disability-rights principles, including the UN
        Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. Quality of life will
        not be treated as a condition that every participant must “fix.” Instead, the
        research will examine whether systems and supports become more responsive,
        accessible, and controllable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="aims-heading">
      <h2 id="aims-heading">2. Aim, objectives, and research questions</h </h2>

      <h3>Aim</h3>
      <p>
        To co-design and evaluate a community support package that improves meaningful
        everyday outcomes for disabled people, with participants retaining control over
        their goals, data, and interpretation of results.
      </p>

      <h3>Objectives</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Identify the everyday barriers and supports that matter most to participants.</li>
        <li>Co-design and deliver peer-led, practical support in 12 communities.</li>
        <li>Measure changes in quality of life, autonomy, participation, belonging, and safety.</li>
        <li>Assess accessibility, acceptability, implementation burden, and cost.</li>
        <li>Produce an open toolkit for disability-led organizations, public agencies, and policymakers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Research questions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Does the constable package improve participant-defined quality-of-life outcomes?</li>
        <li>Which components are most useful, acceptable, and sustainable?</li>
        <li>How do outcomes differ by disability type, gender, ethnicity, age, location, communication need, and other relevant characteristics?</li>
        <li>What barriers to participation, safety, and autonomy remain unaddressed?</li>
        <li>What does the intervention cost per meaningful outcome achieved?</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Primary hypothesis</h3>
      <p>
        Communities receiving the constable package early will show greater improvement
        in participant-defined goals and self-reported quality of life than communities
        receiving it after the delayed-rollout period.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="intervention-heading">
      <h2 id="intervention-heading">3. Proposed intervention</h2>
      <p>
        Each community will adapt the package through a local co-design group. A national
        core team will provide training and a small unrestricted budget, while local
        disability-led partners will decide which elements are most appropriate.
      </p>

      <h3>Peer access navigators</h3>
      <p>
        Paid disabled community members will help participants identify and address
        access barriers. Navigators may accompany people to appointments, help prepare
        accessible information, support self-advocacy, and connect people with peer networks.
        They will not act as case managers or replace participants’ decision-making.
      </p>

      <h3>Barrier-removal microgrants</h3>
      <p>
        Each community will receive grants of up to an agreed local amount for practical
        changes selected by participants. Examples include accessible transport, captioning,
        sign-language interpretation, portable ramps, quiet meeting space, personal assistance,
        or modifications to digital services.
      </p>

      <h3>Accessible local environments</h3>
      <p>
        Community partners will conduct access audits with disabled residents and work
        with venues, employers, transport providers, and public agencies to implement
        improvements. The focus is on removing barriers rather than requiring disabled
        people to adapt.
      </p>

      <h3>Participant-designed action plans</h3>
      <p>
        Each participant may identify one or more goals, such as attending a community
        class, travelling independently, re-entering employment, making a new social
        connection, or obtaining an accessible document. Goals will be selected without
        a predetermined “ideal” outcome.
      </p>

      <h3>Peer connection and self-advocacy</h3>
      <p>
        Participants may join facilitated peer groups, workshops, or one-to-one
        conversations in person, by telephone, or online. Content and format will be
        co-designed locally, with accessible materials and communication support.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="design-heading">
      <h2 id="design-heading">4. Study design and participants</h2>

      <h3>Design</h3>
      <p>
        The project will use a mixed-methods, community-cluster, delayed-rollout design.
        Following co-design, 12 communities will be matched in pairs and randomly assigned
        to early implementation or a nine-month delayed start. All communities will
        ultimately receive the intervention.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data will be collected at baseline, six months, 12 months, and 24 months.
        Community-level implementation data and qualitative interviews will help explain
        how and why outcomes differ.
      </p>

      <h3>Participants</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Approximately 720 disabled people, with about 60 participants per community.</li>
        <li>Self-identification of disability will be the eligibility criterion; diagnosis will not be required.</li>
        <li>Recruitment will occur through disability-led organizations, primary care and social-care pathways, accessible housing organizations, education and employment services, and direct self-referral.</li>
        <li>At least 50% of participants will be from groups currently underrepresented in research, with active outreach to people who use augmentative and alternative communication, people with intellectual disabilities, older disabled people, disabled people from minority ethnic communities, disabled LGBTQ+ people, and disabled people in rural areas.</li>
        <li>Support people may participate as research team members, but proxy responses will never be treated as the participant’s own views without explicit authorization and clear labeling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Co-design phase</h3>
      <p>
        Each community will establish a paid participant advisory group. The groups will
        define local priorities, review recruitment and consent materials, select outcome
        measures, advise on data interpretation, and set expectations for feedback.
        Findings from the co-design phase will guide the final intervention and protocol.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="outcomes-heading">
      <h2 id="outcomes-heading">5. Outcomes and measurement</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Proposed outcomes and examples of measurement approaches</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Outcome category</th>
              <th scope="col">Measures</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Primary outcomes</td>
              <td>
                Participant-defined quality-of-life goals assessed with Goal Attainment
                Scaling and a brief self-reported quality-of-life rating.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Secondary outcomes</td>
              <td>
                Autonomy, community participation, belonging, safety, access to services,
                unwanted administrative or institutional contact, loneliness, and service burden.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Implementation outcomes</td>
              <td>
                Reach, uptake, fidelity to co-designed principles, participant burden,
                acceptability, staff capacity, and the number and type of barriers resolved.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Qualitative outcomes</td>
              <td>
                Interviews, focus groups, participant diaries, and optional photovoice
                exploring changes in control, confidence, connection, and daily life.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Economic outcomes</td>
              <td>
                Cost per meaningful goal achieved and cost per resolved access barrier.
                Quality-of-life outcomes will not be converted into QALYs or used to
                assign monetary value to disabled people.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Accessible measurement</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Participants may complete measures online, by phone, in person, on paper, or with communication support.</li>
        <li>Questionnaires will be available in plain language and Easy Read, with captioned video, audio, large print, and sign-language interpretation where requested.</li>
        <li>Validated measures will be selected according to participant needs rather than requiring everyone to use the same instrument.</li>
        <li>Measures will be pilot-tested with a diverse accessibility panel before full deployment.</li>
        <li>Participants may pause, skip, or stop any question without losing their other responses.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="analysis-heading">
      <h2 id="analysis-heading">6. Data analysis and learning</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Quantitative analysis:</strong> Intention-to-treat multilevel models will
          compare changes over time between early and delayed-rollout communities while
          accounting for repeated observations within participants and communities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reporting:</strong> Results will include effect sizes, confidence
          intervals, response distributions, and participant-defined outcomes, rather
          than relying only on statistical significance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Qualitative analysis:</strong> Researchers and paid community
          analysts will conduct thematic and participatory analysis. Local advisory
          groups will help check interpretations against the original accounts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Equity analysis:</strong> Results will be examined across relevant
          demographic and disability characteristics. Small groups will be suppressed
          to protect confidentiality.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Missing data:</strong> Reasons for missingness will be reported.
          Unanswered subjective questions will not be treated as low quality of life.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learning system:</strong> Quarterly review meetings will identify
          implementation problems. Protocol changes that affect outcome measurement will
          be approved and registered before further data analysis.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="atlantis-heading">
      <h2 id="atlantis-heading">7. The atlantis research commons</h2>
      <p>
        atlantis will be a secure, accessible online and offline research environment.
        It will combine participant communication, data collection, accessible document
        delivery, a community knowledge hub, and governance tools. It will be built to
        WCAG 2.2 AA and tested with screen-reader, keyboard, switch, magnification,
        and cognitive-accessibility users.
      </p>

      <h3>Core features</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Plain-language study information, consent forms, and Easy Read summaries.</li>
        <li>Audio, video, captioned, large-print, and sign-language formats.</li>
        <li>Telephone, postal, paper, and facilitated response options for people who cannot or prefer not to use the platform.</li>
        <li>Participant-controlled consent settings, including separate choices for quotation, reuse, and future contact.</li>
        <li>Accessible data dashboards that show participants their own information and progress.</li>
        <li>Role-based access, encryption, security audits, and clear retention and deletion rules.</li>
        <li>An accessible community knowledge hub featuring participant-approved resources and locally produced guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Data governance</h3>
      <p>
        atlantis will collect only the information required for the research. Personal
        data will not be sold or shared with housing, immigration, policing, or benefit
        agencies without separate, explicit consent. A participant governance group
        will review requests for secondary data use. Public datasets will contain
        de-identified or aggregated information only, with small-cell suppression.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ethics-heading">
      <h2 id="ethics-heading">8. Ethics, inclusion, and safeguarding</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          An independent ethics board will include disabled researchers and community
          advocates. The board will approve accessible consent and information materials.
        </li>
        <li>
          Consent will be an ongoing process. Capacity to consent will not be assumed
          from a diagnosis, and communication support will be offered without penalty.
        </li>
        <li>
          Participants and co-researchers will be paid for interviews, co-design,
          advisory work, and other substantial contributions. Travel, care, access,
          and communication costs will be covered separately.
        </li>
        <li>
          Participants may stop any activity without giving a reason. Data already
          provided may be withdrawn up to the point of anonymized analysis, subject to
          legal and ethical requirements.
        </li>
        <li>
          A confidential complaints and safeguarding process will be available in
          multiple formats. Research staff will receive training in disability
          discrimination, coercion, communication rights, and safe reporting.
        </li>
        <li>
          The project will not collect or share data that could expose participants to
          punitive systems without a clear legal basis, informed consent, and a
          participant-approved purpose.
        </li>
        <li>
          An independent disability advisory panel will conduct quarterly safeguarding
          and inclusion reviews and can recommend suspension of a study activity.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="governance-heading">
      <h2 id="governance-heading">9. Governance and project team</h2>
      <p>
        The project will be governed by a steering council in which at least 60% of voting
        members are disabled people or paid representatives of disability-led
        organizations. A separate participant advisory council will have authority
        over research priorities, data governance, and the public interpretation of results.
      </p>

      <h3>Core roles</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Project director and co-directors:</strong> one disabled and one community-services lead.</li>
        <li><strong>Paid community co-researchers:</strong> recruited and trained within participating communities.</li>
        <li><strong>Research methods team:</strong> disability-inclusive quantitative and qualitative researchers.</li>
        <li><strong>Implementation team:</strong> disability-led partner organizations, peer navigators, and local commissioners.</li>
        <li><strong>atlantis development and security team:</strong> accessibility specialists, software engineers, data stewards, and privacy professionals.</li>
        <li><strong>Communications and knowledge team:</strong> writers, translators, sign-language interpreters, and accessible-format specialists.</li>
        <li><strong>Independent oversight:</strong> ethics, safeguarding, data governance, and participant advisory panels.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="timeline-heading">
      <h2 id="timeline-heading">10. Work plan</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="timeline">
          <caption>Indicative 30-month schedule</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Period</th>
              <th scope="col">Activities</th>
