    <section aria-labelledby="risk-heading">
      <h2 id="risk-heading">12. Risks and mitigation</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Principal project risks and planned responses</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Risk</th>
              <th scope="col">Mitigation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Low participation or unequal representation</td>
              <td>Paid community researchers, trusted partner organizations, accessible transport, non-digital participation, and monitored recruitment data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Research burden or fatigue</td>
              <td>Short modules, flexible scheduling, breaks, capped contact time, and permission to skip questions or activities.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Digital exclusion</td>
              <td>Offer telephone, paper, in-person, and facilitated data collection alongside atlantis.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Support between communities affects the comparison</td>
              <td>Record outside services and peer activity, use matched communities, and interpret findings with process data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Privacy or safeguarding concerns</td>
              <td>Data minimization, strict role-based access, secure infrastructure, independent oversight, and accessible reporting channels.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Short-term funding ends before services are sustained</td>
              <td>Co-design a handover and resourcing plan with local agencies and disability-led organizations from the beginning.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="impact-heading">
      <h2 id="impact-heading">13. Expected impact and success criteria</h2>
      <p>
        The project will assess success through both research outcomes and the quality
        of the research process. The participant advisory council will approve the final
        success framework before the main outcome analysis.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>At least 85% of participants report that the research respected their choices, communication needs, privacy, and time.</li>
        <li>At least 75% of participants complete two or more follow-up data-collection points, with no major demographic group more than 15 percentage points below the overall rate.</li>
        <li>Early-rollout communities show a pre-registered, participant-meaningful improvement in at least one primary outcome.</li>
        <li>Local advisory groups document practical barriers resolved and changes in access, autonomy, participation, belonging, or safety.</li>
        <li>At least ten public agencies or disability-led organizations commit to adopting or testing components of the model.</li>
        <li>All public research outputs are available in at least plain-language, accessible digital, and Easy Read formats, with additional formats available on request.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The final outputs will include an open implementation toolkit, participant-led
        resources, policy briefs, peer-led training materials, a public methods report,
        and a participant-approved plain-language summary.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="principle-heading">
      <h2 id="principle-heading">14. Expected contribution</h2>
      <p>
        constable will test a practical, rights-based model of support in which disabled
        people define meaningful outcomes and hold real decision-making power. The
        atlantis research commons will address a persistent weakness in disability
        research: inaccessible systems of recruitment, consent, measurement, and data
        governance.
      </p>
      <p>
        Even if the intervention produces only modest average effects, the project may
        identify which combinations of peer support, practical resources, and institutional
        change produce the greatest benefits for whom and under what circumstances.
        The resulting evidence and tools will support disability-led organizations and
        public systems seeking to move from access as an aspiration to access as an
        everyday, measurable, and enforceable reality.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="inner">
      <p><strong>Project working names:</strong> constable and atlantis</p>
      <p>
        This proposal treats disabled people as participants, collaborators, researchers,
        and decision-makers throughout the project lifecycle.
      </p>
    </div>
  </footer>
